analysis:
Reviewed the ServingReadiness.swift refactor, the new ProviderOperationalMessage case, and the ProviderLoop+Serve.swift event hook.

ServingReadiness is diagnostics only (file header, line 9); the coordinator still owns routing and authorization. The changes add:
- an `.unconfirmed` verdict;
- `refusalMaxAge` (60 s);
- a `justConnected` grace for refusals;
- a `routed` override: a coordinator request received after the latest status is shown as authorized (lines 77-79, 113).

None of this alters the TB-011 / T-052 / T-053 enforcement paths. The verdict is not a grant, and the coordinator's explicit `untrusted`/`offline` status and an expired App Attest lease still return `.blocked` before the routed override is considered (lines 89-111). One residual point: `lastRequestAt` reflects any `.inferenceRequest` event, so a coordinator-routed request is treated as evidence of authorization. A coordinator is trusted, so this does not widen adversary capability, and the display cannot be turned into serving permission. Nothing visible makes it a security regression.

The added log message in ProviderLogger.swift line 149 is a fixed string with no associated values, so the closed public log vocabulary (the privacy contract at lines 118-121) is preserved.

The hook in ProviderLoop+Serve.swift (patch lines 429-430) only calls `noteCoordinatorRequest()`. I could not see that function's body, nor the `DaemonState.CoordinatorConnection.lastRequestAt` definition or where it is persisted. A reviewer should confirm that it records only a timestamp and no request-derived content. The `ProviderLoop.superviseServingAuthorization` re-registration logic that consumes the verdict was not supplied, so whether the new `.unconfirmed`/`.pending` verdicts change re-registration behavior is unverified (availability, not confidentiality). The `refusalMaxAge` stale-refusal path could reduce operator alerts for genuine refusals, which is a diagnostic UX trade-off. I did not treat any of these as security findings.
